+/* SCUS-94154 only: the per-polygon screen test, widened for native-wide.
+ *
+ * Crash 2's renderers drop a polygon when every projected vertex lies past the
+ * same edge of the 512x217 screen, with a packed test (B = 0x00D90200, that is
+ * 217<<16 | 512, and s = a GTE SXY word, Y<<16 | X):
+ *
+ *     t8 = ~((s0-B) | (s1-B) | (s2-B)) | (s0 & s1 & s2)
+ *     bltz t8, reject      ; bit 31: all Y above the top, or all below 217
+ *     sll  t8, t8, 16
+ *     bltz t8, reject      ; bit 15: all X < 0, or all X >= 512
+ *
+ * Bit 15 of s is X's sign; bit 15 of s-B is clear exactly when X >= 512 for the
+ * GTE's X range [-1024, 1023].
+ *
+ * Squash widescreen (mode 1) squeezes X inside the GTE, before this test, so
+ * the game already tests the wider view. Native-wide (mode 2) leaves the GTE
+ * alone and draws the extra columns through the GPU draw offset, so the game
+ * still tests [0,512): every polygon lying wholly inside a revealed margin,
+ * [-off,0) or [512,512+off), was dropped and only polygons straddling the old
+ * 4:3 edge reached it. That was the broken edge in native-wide. The level's
+ * draw lists already hold those polygons (NOTES, widescreen part 4), and the
+ * object window (crash2_wide_spawn.h) has nothing to do with it.
+ *
+ * Every branch in the generated code calls psx_check_interrupts_at with the PC
+ * it continues at, so the block holding the X branch is entered, only by
+ * falling through the Y branch, with t8 = mask<<16. There this re-decides X
+ * against the window native-wide shows, [-off, 512+off), and clears bit 31 of
+ * t8 when the polygon reaches into it.
+ *
+ *   - It only turns a reject into a keep, and only for polygons squash mode
+ *     already draws: at the same aspect both accept exactly [-off, 512+off),
+ *     so primitive-buffer and ordering-table use is what squash produces.
+ *   - t8 is scratch. Every kept path writes it before reading it, and the value
+ *     left is one the game itself leaves for a kept polygon.
+ *   - Before changing anything it recomputes the game's own verdict from the
+ *     vertices it read. If that differs from the game's bit, the vertices are
+ *     not the ones tested: it counts a mismatch and leaves the polygon alone.
+ *   - Off (identity) at 4:3, in squash mode and on frames presented 4:3.
+ *
+ * The seven sites (nothing else branches or jumps into these blocks):
+ *
+ *   0x80041C18  triangle  model polygons (0x80041B80)       SXY0..2
+ *   0x800424E0  triangle  world, func_80041E5C               SXY0..2
+ *   0x800427A0  quad      world                              [v1+376], SXY0..2
+ *   0x8004518C  line      0x80044F54                         SXY0..1
+ *   0x80045404  dot       0x80045218, right edge only        $a1 (SXY2)
+ *   0x80045EC4  triangle  world, second loop                 SXY0..2
+ *   0x800460D0  quad      world, second loop                 [v1+376], SXY0..2
+ *
+ * A quad projects its fourth vertex (RTPS) between reading the first three and
+ * the test, which pushes vertex 0 out of the GTE; both quad paths store it
+ * first with `swc2 SXY0, 376($v1)`. The dot test has no left edge in the game
+ * either (it draws 1x1 dots that start at the point).
+ *
+ * PSX_CRASH2_WIDE_REJECT=0 turns it off; the c2_reject debug command switches
+ * it live and reports the counters, which the heartbeat carries as
+ * "wide_reject". */
